<Project>

    <!-- What every project of the tree is built with, which the build reads for each project of it without a project
         naming it: the language every file is written in, the model of the nullability of the values, the usings which
         a file which names nothing of its own is given, and the four checks which are held to rather than reported.

         A check which is written here rather than in each project is what makes it a convention of the tree: what a
         file may hold is asked of the build once, and a project cannot be added which is built under other rules than
         the ones the files of the tree are written for. -->

    <PropertyGroup>
        <!-- The version of the language which the files of the tree are written in, which is written out rather than
             left at the one the SDK carries: the extension members of C# 14 are what the weaver is written with, and a
             build which read them under another version would report them rather than what they hold. -->
        <LangVersion>14</LangVersion>

        <!-- The model of the nullability of the values, which every project is written for: a reference which may be
             absent is the one which says so. -->
        <Nullable>enable</Nullable>

        <!-- The usings which a file which names none of its own is given, which are the namespaces the files of the tree
             are written against: the names of the framework are read without a file writing them out, and only what is
             not one of them is named. -->
        <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>

        <!-- The comments of the sources are written beside the assembly they describe, so that whoever refers to it reads
             the comment of a member where the call is written. A member which carries none is reported by the build: a
             library of this tree is published with its comments, and one which is missing is a member a reader cannot
             find out about. The tests document nothing of their own - what a test asserts is its name and what it
             reports - and they turn that report off for themselves. -->
        <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

        <!-- The style which the editor of this repository is configured with is enforced by the build, so that a file
             which is written in another one is told where it stands rather than where it is read. -->
        <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>

        <!-- What those checks report is what the build holds to: a warning of the analysis, of the style or of the
             compiler is a file which does not meet the conventions of the tree, and the build which produced it is a
             build which is not to be taken as one which passed. The first of the two is what the property above turns
             on for the analyses, and it is the second which makes it a rule of the build: a property which names the
             analyses alone holds only the diagnostics which are already enabled, which is none of them in a tree which
             is written for no analysis in particular. -->
        <CodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors>true</CodeAnalysisTreatWarningsAsErrors>
        <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
